WEBVTT

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:00.860
Harvest-now,

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:05.490
decrypt-later risk exists because some encrypted data may remain valuable long after it is

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.160
captured.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.360
The attacker in this model may not be able to decrypt the data today.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.810
The concern is future exposure if cryptographic assumptions change,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.200
implementation weaknesses become exploitable,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.135
or advances such as cryptographically relevant quantum computing affect public-key

00:00:19.135 --> 00:00:19.670
protections.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.740
The highest concern is long-lived sensitive data, not every byte of encrypted traffic.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.100
The strongest preparation starts with data classification and data flow mapping.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.245
Teams need to know what information must remain confidential,

00:00:30.245 --> 00:00:33.185
how long it remains sensitive, where it moves, where it is stored,

00:00:33.185 --> 00:00:35.181
and which systems or third parties handle it.

00:00:35.181 --> 00:00:40.050
Defenders should think beyond servers to include TLS endpoints, VPNs, APIs, email,

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.030
file transfers, backups, replication, service-to-service traffic, mobile applications,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.200
logs, archives, and vendor-managed paths.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.300
Cryptographic inventory and dependency mapping connect the data risk to real engineering

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:51.610
work.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.775
The inventory should identify algorithms, protocols, certificates, libraries,

00:00:55.775 --> 00:01:00.280
key management systems, hardware security modules, cloud services, third-party products,

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.240
owners, data protected, lifetime, and migration status.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.560
From there, teams can prioritize based on sensitivity, lifetime, exposure, volume,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.725
mission importance, vendor readiness, and migration difficulty.

00:01:10.725 --> 00:01:11.785
The goal is not fear.

00:01:11.785 --> 00:01:15.995
The goal is reducing long-term confidentiality risk before emergency migration becomes

00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:16.655
necessary.

00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:21.415
Strong programs build crypto agility, use standards-based migration paths, test changes,

00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:25.230
coordinate with vendors, reduce unnecessary retention, document assumptions,

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.193
and communicate clearly with leadership.

00:01:27.193 --> 00:01:30.625
Harvest-now, decrypt-later planning is ultimately a resilience exercise:

00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:35.040
know what must stay confidential, know what protects it, and prepare to adapt safely.
